  },
  "paragraphs": [
    {
      "num": "01",
      "en": "{Dogs greet} each other nose-first, as it were— {sniffing} each other from {fore} to {aft} .People are not quite so open about the process of sniffing each other out.But the {size} of the {perfume industry} suggests {scent} is important in human {relations} , too.There is also {evidence} that human {beings} can {infer} kinship ( (亲戚关系) ), {deduce} emotional states and even {detect} disease {via} the sense of smell.",
      "cn": "可以说， 狗 会先用鼻子互相 打招呼 ——从 前 到 后 地把对方 闻 一遍。而人类在相互了解彼此的过程中可不会这么直白。不过， 香水产业 的 规模 却表明， 气味 在人际 关系 中也很重要。还有 证据 表明， 人类 可以 通过 嗅觉 推断 亲缘关系， 推测 情绪状态，甚至 检测 出疾病。"
    },
    {
      "num": "02",
      "en": "Now, researchers from the Weizmann {Institute} of Science in Israel have gone a step further.They think they have shown, {admittedly} in a {fairly} small {sample} of individuals, that friends actually smell {alike} .They have also shown that this is probably the case from the beginning, with people picking friends at least {partly} on the {basis} of body odor ( (气味) ), rather than the body odors of people who become friends {subsequently} converging ( (趋同) ).",
      "cn": "现在，以色列魏茨曼科学 研究院 的研究者已经又向前迈进了一步。他们认为自己的研究已经说明，能做朋友的人确实闻起来 很像 —— 当然 ，研究的 样本 仅包括 相当 少数的个体。他们还证明，这种情况很可能从一开始就是如此。也就是说，至少 在一定程度上 ，人们是 根据 气味来挑选朋友的，而不是在朋友关系建立 之后 ，气味才逐渐趋同。"
    },
    {
      "num": "03",
      "en": "They started their research by testing the odors of 20 {pairs} of same-sex friends who were not in a {romantic} relationship.They did this using an {electronic nose} and also two groups of specially employed human “smellers”.The e-nose employed a set of {gas sensors} to {assess T-shirts worn} by participants.One group of human smellers were given pairs of these shirts and asked to {rate} how similar they smelt.Those in the other group were asked to rate the odors of individual T-shirts on five {subjective dimensions} : {pleasantness} , {intensity} , {attractiveness} , {competence} and warmth.All three approaches {yielded} the same result.The T-shirts of friends smelt more similar to each other than the T-shirts of {strangers} .Friends, in other words, do {indeed} smell alike.",
      "cn": "他们首先对20 对 处于非 恋爱 关系的同性朋友身上的气味进行了测试。他们使用了一个 电子鼻 ，以及两组专门雇用的人类“嗅探员”来进行测试。电子鼻使用一组 气体传感器 来 评估 参与者 穿 过的 T恤衫 。一组人类“嗅探员”拿到成对的T恤衫，并按要求 评定 它们的气味相似程度。另一组人则被要求通过五个 主观维度 对单件T恤衫的气味进行评分，包括 愉悦度 、 浓烈程度 、 吸引力 、 能力 和温暖感。这三种测试方法都 得出 了相同的结果。与 陌生人 的T恤衫相比，朋友的T恤衫闻起来更相似。换句话说，朋友之间 确实 有类似的气味。"
    },
    {
      "num": "04",
      "en": "Why scent might play a role in forming friendships remains obscure.Other qualities related to being friends, including age, appearance, education, {religion} and race, are either immediately obvious or rapidly become so.But while some individuals have strong and {noticeable} body odor, many—at least since the use of {soap} has become {widespread} —do not.It is {present} .But it is {subconscious} .",
      "cn": "气味为何可以在友谊的形成过程中发挥作用仍不得而知。与成为朋友相关的其他特质，包括年龄、外貌、受教育程度、 宗教信仰 和种族，要么是一目了然，要么很快就能知晓。然而，尽管有些人有强烈而 明显的 体味，但许多人——至少自从 肥皂广泛 使用以来——并非如此。体味是 存在的 ，不过它是 潜意识的 。 5NoOncib/NjiX02BpMIzP4m+378Hw1xTRxUdPaP/PFjKUFq4K2G7tp6ZLYoCE601yW1W39VMdLmovFdSHs4nVQ=="
    }
  ],
